Helios vkd3d-proton fork: ECL fences on NVK on RM (HE12 v4 ordering)

Conduit guest/windows/docs/rm-fence-marker.md; the UMD's Nvk12EclSync=2.
Applied after 0003.

On NVK the UMD ordered the runtime's WDDM context behind each
ExecuteCommandLists by waiting for the batch on the CPU, which serialises
CPU and GPU (about one host round trip, ~0.75 ms, per batch). With an ECL
fence the UMD instead submits an HE12 v4 record naming an RM fence that
fires when the batch has executed: the KMD withholds the packet's DMA
completion until then, so the runtime's fence signals and presents follow
the real work, while the next batch's admission (SignalAtSubmission) fires
at once.

- helios_vkd3d_execute_command_lists_rm(...): helios_vkd3d_execute_command_lists
  plus *rm_fence (an RM fence handle the caller owns) and *rm_value. Under
  queue_lock, at the commit, the batch reserves the next value of its
  queue's ECL timeline (NVK helios_icd_interface version 7
  ecl_fence_reserve, keyed by the d3d12_command_queue, so queues sharing a
  VkQueue never interleave); the fence is made outside the lock
  (ecl_fence_create). Both 0 when the ICD has no ECL fences.
- The submission worker signals the value (ecl_fence_signal) right after
  the batch's execution signal, in the same Vulkan queue hold. A reserved
  value whose fence could not be made is signalled anyway.
- The ICD table is resolved once per process from the module behind the
  device's vkWaitSemaphores (the NVK device is made on the ICD directly).
  helios_icd_interface.h is a verbatim copy of Conduit's.
- helios_vkd3d_ecl_fence_stats(out, count): signals, commit-to-signal sum
  and max (us), signal failures, values reserved, fences made, fence
  creates failed, and a commit-to-signal histogram (<50 us, <200 us, <1 ms,
  <2 ms, <5 ms, <20 ms, >=20 ms).
- helios_vkd3d.def exports both.

